            "q": "A 68-year-old on doxorubicin chemotherapy (cardiotoxic) develops new heart failure (EF 38% down from 55%), atrial fibrillation with RVR (HR 168), and dyspnea. Labs: K 3.2 (low), Mg 1.3 (low), Ca 8.1 (low), Phos 2.8 (low). All four electrolytes ABNORMAL simultaneously. What is the diagnosis that explains SIMULTANEOUS electrolyte derangement?",
            "a": "DOXORUBICIN-INDUCED CARDIOTOXICITY creating SECONDARY MULTI-ELECTROLYTE WASTING from cardiac dysfunction — massive natriuresis/kaliuresis from heart failure + diuretic therapy + activation of RAAS + aldosterone excess from low cardiac output; additionally: doxorubicin directly causes electrolyte wasting (damages kidney tubules) and blocks magnesium/potassium reuptake; MANAGEMENT: (1) ACE-I/ARB (blocks aldosterone excess), (2) Beta-blocker for rate control + cardioprotection, (3) AGGRESSIVE electrolyte repletion: IV potassium (goal K >4.0), IV magnesium (goal Mg >2.0), oral calcium + phosphate, (4) Discontinue doxorubicin (cardiotoxicity ≥50% EF drop = contraindicated), (5) Consider other cardioprotective agents (carvedilol for HF management)",
            "rationale": "Chemotherapy-induced cardiomyopathy triggers cascade of kidney compensatory responses (natriuresis, kaliuresis, aldosterone excess) trying to maintain cardiac output but worsening electrolyte status. Additionally: doxorubicin directly toxic to tubular reabsorption. Management: (1) ACE-I/ARB essential (reduce aldosterone), (2) Beta-blocker (carvedilol has additional cardioprotective antioxidant effects), (3) AGGRESSIVE electrolyte repletion (K >4.0, Mg >2.0), (4) Discontinue doxorubicin (cumulative cardiotoxicity), (5) Monitor EF with serial echocardiography, (6) Consider heart transplant workup if EF remains severely depressed."
