            "id": "elec-200-1",
            "q": "A 52-year-old with panhypopituitarism (pit apoplexy 6 months ago, on thyroid + cortisol replacement) develops ACUTE HYPONATREMIA (Na 104) with seizures. He's on SIADH-like therapy accidentally (vasopressin given instead of desmopressin for diabetic insipidus diagnosis). What is the critical management dilemma?",
            "a": "MULTI-PROBLEM: (1) Panhypopituitarism patient ALSO has central hypothyroidism + cortisol deficiency, (2) Accidentally given vasopressin causing SIADH, (3) Acute hyponatremia with seizures (Na 104 = LIFE-THREATENING); MANAGEMENT: (1) Hypertonic saline for acute symptomatic hyponatremia (seizures override usual restrictions), (2) STOP vasopressin immediately, (3) Assess thyroid/cortisol status (hypothyroidism + hypocortisolism worsen hyponatremia response), (4) May need HYDROCORTISONE BOLUS if cortisol deficiency contributing (cortisol needed for free water excretion), (5) Free water restriction once seizures controlled",
            "rationale": "Panhypopituitary patients have complex electrolyte management needs: (1) Central hypothyroidism impairs free water clearance, (2) Cortisol deficiency impairs free water excretion, (3) Accidental vasopressin → SIADH on top of already impaired water handling = catastrophic hyponatremia. Management: (1) Hypertonic saline for acute symptomatic (seizures), (2) STOP vasopressin, (3) Assess cortisol level (may need hydrocortisone 50-100mg IV for emergency), (4) Ensure levothyroxine adequate dose (improves free water clearance), (5) Once seizures controlled: free water restriction."
