            "q": "A 45-year-old with SYNDROME OF INAPPROPRIATE ANTIDIURESIS (SIAD) from SCLC is treated with VAPTANS (tolvaptan 15mg daily). After 5 days: Na improved to 125 (from 108). BUT then develops: acute confusion, hallucinations, disorientation, tremor. Labs: Na NOW 136 (overcorrected), glucose 45 (severe hypoglycemia), thiamine deficiency suspected. What is the diagnosis that bridges hyponatremia treatment + neurologic complication?",
            "a": "OSMOTIC DEMYELINATION SYNDROME (ODS) early stage + WERNICKE ENCEPHALOPATHY from alcohol-related thiamine deficiency (often concurrent with SCLC/chronic illness); additionally: OVERVAPTAN CORRECTION (Na increased from 108 to 136 = 28 mEq/L correction, TOO MUCH TOO FAST) causing demyelination + iatrogenic DI from vaptans (can cause new hypernatremia); MANAGEMENT: (1) STOP vaptans immediately (causing overcorrection), (2) THIAMINE 100mg IV immediately (Wernicke emergency), (3) Allow Na to drift back down slowly (DO NOT rush — prevent further ODS), (4) Glucose administration (hypoglycemia treating), (5) ICU monitoring, (6) MRI brain (confirm ODS + assess for Wernicke changes), (7) Neuro rehabilitation"
