            "q": "A 54-year-old with severe NEPHROTIC SYNDROME (serum albumin 1.2, urine protein 18g/day, Cr 1.8) develops PROFOUND HYPONATREMIA (Na 102) with seizures. He's on aggressive diuretic therapy (furosemide 240mg daily + spironolactone 200mg daily) for edema/ascites management. Additionally: SIADH pattern noted (urine osmolality 680 despite serum osmolality 215 = LOW). What is the PATHOPHYSIOLOGY connecting nephrotic syndrome + SIADH?",
            "a": "NEPHROTIC SYNDROME-INDUCED SIADH from: (1) Massive proteinuria → oncotic pressure loss → perceived 'volume depletion' despite total body water overload (pseudohypovolemia), (2) Baroreceptors sense low plasma osmolality + perceive volume depletion → ADH release (SIADH) → worsens hyponatremia, (3) Diuretics worsen volume depletion perception → amplify ADH release; MANAGEMENT PARADOX: (1) Hyponatremia suggests volume overload (needs diuretics), BUT treating with diuretics worsens hyponatremia via ADH activation (paradoxical worsening), (2) Solution: REDUCE DIURETICS (counterintuitive but necessary), (3) Fluid RESTRICTION (unusual in nephrotic edema, but necessary for hyponatremia), (4) Hypertonic saline for acute symptomatic (seizures), (5) Treat underlying nephrotic syndrome aggressively (immunosuppression, NSAIDs if not contraindicated, ACE-I), (6) Once nephrotic syndrome improves: hyponatremia typically resolves"
