            "q": "A 71-year-old on chronic metformin (Cr baseline 1.2, eGFR 58) undergoes emergent abdominal surgery for perforated peptic ulcer. Intra-operatively: receives 4L normal saline + 2 units PRBC, hypotensive briefly (SBP 78), but recovers with fluids. Post-op day 1: Cr rises to 2.8, lactate elevated (3.2, normal <2), patient altered. What is the CRITICAL MISTAKE and management adjustment?",
            "a": "METFORMIN-ASSOCIATED LACTIC ACIDOSIS (MALA) precipitated by intra-operative hypotension causing transient AKI + metformin accumulation in kidneys (metformin renally cleared, reduced clearance in AKI → accumulation) — metformin should have been HELD before surgery in patient with reduced renal function (eGFR 58 is borderline, high risk for MALA if AKI develops); EMERGENCY management: (1) STOP metformin immediately, (2) AGGRESSIVE IV fluids (normal saline) + sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize (promotes lactate clearance), (3) EMERGENT hemodialysis (MALA management — dialysis removes both lactate + metformin), (4) Support BP aggressively (vasopressors if needed — hypotension worsens lactic acidosis), (5) Monitor lactate closely (goal <2), (6) Treat underlying shock (post-op bleeding? infection?)"
